<section class="panel">
	<div class="wrap">
		<div class="eyebrow">Route 1 &middot; recommended</div>
		<h1>Flash the ready image</h1>
		<p class="lede">One download, everything inside: kernel, hardware fixes, the
		six own applications, browser, mail, maps, photos. About 1&nbsp;GB
		compressed.</p>
		<p><a class="btn" href="https://github.com/utsugi-pmos/utsugi-pmaports/releases/latest/download/@IMG@.xz">Download @IMG@.xz &mdash; 1.2 GB</a></p>
		<div class="note"><i></i><div>
			<b>Four steps come before the flashing, and the first one takes a week.</b>
			Unlock the bootloader at Xiaomi, find out which screen your phone has,
			put U-Boot on it, and get <code>fastboot</code> on your PC. They are all
			below, in order.
		</div></div>
	</div>
</section>

<section>
	<div class="wrap">
		<div class="h"><span>01</span><h2>Unlock the bootloader at Xiaomi</h2></div>
		<p>The phone refuses to boot anything but MIUI until Xiaomi lets it. It
		needs a Mi account, <b>seven days of waiting</b>, and it erases the phone.
		Start it today; the rest of this page takes twenty minutes once it is
		done.</p>
		<p>It does not need Windows: <code>miunlock</code>
		(<a href="https://github.com/offici5l/MiUnlockTool">MiUnlockTool</a>) does it
		from Linux.</p>
		<p><a class="btn" href="unlock.html">The unlock, step by step &rarr;</a></p>

		<div class="h" style="margin-top:34px"><span>02</span><h2>Find out which screen you have</h2></div>
		<p>The POCO X3 NFC shipped with two panels, <b>Tianma</b> and <b>Huaxing</b>,
		and U-Boot comes as one build per panel. The wrong one boots fine and draws
		nothing &mdash; a black screen, not a brick.</p>
		<p>From MIUI, with USB debugging on (the developer options you turned on
		while unlocking) and <code>adb</code> installed:</p>
		<div class="sh">
			<div class="bar"><i></i><span>IN A TERMINAL ON YOUR PC (LINUX)</span></div>
			<pre>adb shell cat /proc/cmdline | tr ' ' '\n' | grep -i dsi</pre>
		</div>
		<p>Xiaomi's kernel names the panel in there: the line carries
		<code>tianma</code> or <code>huaxing</code>. If nothing useful comes out,
		flash one and look at the screen &mdash; black means the other one, and you
		can reflash as often as you like.</p>

		<div class="h" style="margin-top:34px"><span>03</span><h2>Get fastboot on your PC</h2></div>
		<div class="scroll">
		<table>
			<tr><th>Debian/Ubuntu</th><td><code>apt install android-sdk-platform-tools</code></td></tr>
			<tr><th>Arch</th><td><code>pacman -S android-tools</code></td></tr>
			<tr><th>Fedora</th><td><code>dnf install android-tools</code></td></tr>
			<tr><th>Alpine</th><td><code>apk add android-tools</code></td></tr>
			<tr><th>Windows / macOS</th><td>platform-tools from developer.android.com;
			on Windows also the Google USB driver. The
			<a href="index.html">front page</a> has the commands for both.</td></tr>
		</table>
		</div>
		<p>And a <b>data</b> cable, straight into the computer if you can: hubs cause
		half the "device not found".</p>

		<div class="h" style="margin-top:34px"><span>04</span><h2>U-Boot on <code>boot</code></h2></div>
		<p>This port does not boot a kernel from the <code>boot</code> partition
		&mdash; it boots U-Boot from there, and U-Boot loads the system from
		<code>userdata</code>. So <code>boot</code> is written once, with U-Boot, and
		never again.</p>
		<p>Take the image for your panel from
		<a href="https://github.com/sm7150-mainline/u-boot/releases">sm7150-mainline/u-boot</a>,
		put the phone in fastboot &mdash; off, then volume-down + power &mdash; and:</p>
		<div class="sh">
			<div class="bar"><i></i><span>IN A TERMINAL ON YOUR PC (LINUX)</span></div>
			<pre>sudo fastboot devices        <b># must print your phone</b>
sudo fastboot erase dtbo     <i># MIUI's overlay would fight U-Boot</i>
sudo fastboot flash boot u-boot-sm7150-xiaomi-surya-tianma.img
sudo fastboot reboot</pre>
		</div>
		<p>Power on: you should get a text console complaining it has nothing to
		boot. That complaint is the good outcome &mdash; there is no system on
		<code>userdata</code> yet. A screen that stays black means the other panel.</p>
		<p>Already running postmarketOS on this phone? Then U-Boot is there and this
		step is done.</p>
	</div>
</section>

<section class="panel">
	<div class="wrap">
		<div class="h"><span>05</span><h2>Put the phone in fastboot</h2></div>
		<p>Turn it off. Then hold <b>volume-down + power</b> until the screen says
		<i>fastboot</i>, and plug the cable in. If the phone already runs this system,
		<code>sudo reboot-to-bootloader</code> on the phone does the same thing.</p>
		<div class="sh">
			<div class="bar"><i></i><span>ON YOUR PC</span></div>
			<pre>sudo fastboot devices
<i># prints something like:  83d9a95b   fastboot</i>
<i># nothing? another cable, another port, and check the phone is really</i>
<i># in fastboot and not just off.</i></pre>
		</div>
		<p><code>sudo</code> because without udev rules the device belongs to root.</p>
	</div>
</section>

<section>
	<div class="wrap">
		<div class="h"><span>06</span><h2>Flash it</h2></div>
		<p><b>Your data is gone from here on:</b> this writes the whole system
		partition.</p>
		<div class="sh">
			<div class="bar"><i></i><span>ON YOUR PC</span></div>
			<pre>xz -d @IMG@.xz            <i># a half-downloaded file fails right here</i>

sudo fastboot flash userdata @IMG@   <b># userdata. never boot.</b>
sudo fastboot reboot</pre>
		</div>
		<p>The phone boots to the desktop. First boot takes a minute longer.</p>
		<div class="note"><i></i><div>
			<b>Log in and change the password.</b>
			The image ships user <code>user</code> with password <code>147147</code>,
			because an image has to have one and it is printed on this page &mdash; so
			anybody who can read knows the password of a phone that has not changed it.
			Change it in <b>Settings &rarr; Users</b>, or in a terminal:
			<code>passwd</code>.
			<br><br>
			The phone will remind you: on every boot it checks whether the password is
			still the one it shipped with and, while it is, says so in a notification.
			It does not store the password to do that &mdash; it takes the salt out of
			your own entry in <code>/etc/shadow</code>, hashes the shipped one with it
			and compares. Change the password and the reminder stops, for good.
		</div></div>
	</div>
</section>

<section>
	<div class="wrap">
		<div class="h"><span>07</span><h2>Then</h2></div>
		<div class="scroll">
		<table>
			<tr><th>Wifi</th><td>Settings, as usual. Nothing is preconfigured.</td></tr>
			<tr><th>Updates</th><td><code>sudo apk upgrade</code>. The repository line
			and the signing key are already inside, kernel included.</td></tr>
			<tr><th>Getting back to fastboot</th><td><code>sudo reboot-to-bootloader</code>.
			<code>systemctl reboot bootloader</code> does not work here.</td></tr>
		</table>
		</div>
		<div class="next">
			<a href="devices.html"><b>Read</b><span>What does not work</span></a>
			<a href="packages.html"><b>See</b><span>What is inside</span></a>
		</div>
	</div>
</section>
